1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,520
No, the title isn't clickbait.

2
00:00:01,600 --> 00:00:04,240
We did actually move T3 chat off of Next.js.

3
00:00:04,640 --> 00:00:08,400
There's a lot of reasons for this, and man, it's been a wild journey.

4
00:00:08,622 --> 00:00:10,960
I know your first question, what did you move to?

5
00:00:11,310 --> 00:00:12,036
Tan stack.

6
00:00:12,398 --> 00:00:24,960
This was quite a PR. Shout out to Julius for all of the work to make this happen, as well as the rest of the team for the 478 comments on his 14,000 lines added and 10,000 lines removed

7
00:00:25,120 --> 00:00:25,476
PR.

8
00:00:25,920 --> 00:00:28,196
Yeah, this one was a doozy.

9
00:00:28,560 --> 00:00:33,120
There has been a lot for us to do and even more to learn as a result of this migration.

10
00:00:33,486 --> 00:00:41,760
And as much as I'm loving Tanstack Start, and believe me, we are loving it, it's a very, very good framework that is a solution to a lot of problems that we and many other developers have.

11
00:00:42,030 --> 00:00:43,920
There are plenty of catches as well.

12
00:00:44,238 --> 00:00:50,084
This was not an easy migration, and the reasons it was hard were not ones any of us would have predicted or expected.

13
00:00:50,670 --> 00:00:58,724
We're at the point where we're patching Tanstack Start in order to add things we need to be exported so we can actually make this work at our size and scale.

14
00:00:59,278 --> 00:01:00,324
It was worth it though.

15
00:01:00,846 --> 00:01:03,040
The reasons why are not the usual.

16
00:01:03,342 --> 00:01:09,200
If you're watching this video, so you can hear me complain about NextJS a whole bunch, you're in the wrong video. This is not going to be that.

17
00:01:09,454 --> 00:01:18,692
If you think we made this move because we are scared of the exploit that I just covered recently, you're probably too bad of a developer to watch my channel, and I would recommend finding another.

18
00:01:19,054 --> 00:01:26,720
But if you're interested in a deep dive on how a company shipping to millions of users thinks about their technology stack and makes changes in order to make the best

19
00:01:26,720 --> 00:01:32,240
possible product and have the best experience for its developers, stay tuned because we have a lot to dive into here.

20
00:01:32,494 --> 00:01:40,036
As you guys know, Vercel is not paying me anymore. And after this, I sincerely doubt they would want to. So let's hear a quick word from today's sponsor and then we'll dive in.

21
00:01:40,270 --> 00:01:41,120
We all know the meme.

22
00:01:41,200 --> 00:01:42,500
Have you checked if it's DNS?

23
00:01:42,720 --> 00:01:45,760
It's so common that DNS problems result in things going down.

24
00:01:45,934 --> 00:01:47,360
But I have a theory about this.

25
00:01:47,598 --> 00:01:49,680
I don't think the problem with DNS is DNS.

26
00:01:49,902 --> 00:01:52,320
I think the problem with DNS is how we interact with it.

27
00:01:52,494 --> 00:01:59,280
These crappy old ancient web interfaces that have a bunch of weird hidden fields trying to make things work is not the way we should be doing things.

28
00:01:59,502 --> 00:02:07,840
In an ideal world, we're programmers. We should be able to program against our DNS. We should be able to write code that takes control of how our domain servers work.

29
00:02:08,142 --> 00:02:10,400
And that's why DN Simple is blowing me away.

30
00:02:10,606 --> 00:02:12,080
They're not yet another registry.

31
00:02:12,462 --> 00:02:13,360
There's so much more than that.

32
00:02:13,582 --> 00:02:18,660
They're focused on being the best possible developer experience for deploying and managing your domains.

33
00:02:18,880 --> 00:02:22,000
Everything from support to the SDKs they provide really shows that.

34
00:02:22,222 --> 00:02:26,960
They have an SDK for every major language, even Elixir, which obviously is something close to my heart.

35
00:02:27,120 --> 00:02:29,520
And the things you can do with them are so cool.

36
00:02:29,838 --> 00:02:37,520
We're talking like four lines of code to create records on your domains. You're trying to set up some vibe coding platform where people can deploy things on a subdomain.

37
00:02:37,680 --> 00:02:40,080
I cannot imagine an easier way to do that than DNSimple.

38
00:02:40,366 --> 00:02:46,880
Whether it's a side project or a big thing, like, you know, the Linux Foundation or the RubyGems ecosystem, all of which run on DNS simple, by the way.

39
00:02:47,086 --> 00:02:47,920
They have you handled.

40
00:02:48,080 --> 00:02:51,200
They even have Terraform configurations. Like, what?

41
00:02:51,470 --> 00:02:56,740
I couldn't fathom having my domains managed through Terraform until now. And it just makes sense.

42
00:02:57,200 --> 00:03:02,800
Like, duh, that's where we should be putting this information. It oh, the amount of like, what?

43
00:03:02,960 --> 00:03:08,080
This is how we should always have been doing it moments. I have had the more I've looked into DN Simple is genuinely hilarious.

44
00:03:08,366 --> 00:03:15,120
And a lot of that's because the team is all engineers. Everybody in marketing and support writes real code and relates to us as developers.

45
00:03:15,438 --> 00:03:20,240
If DNS is causing you problems or holding you back, you really should give them a shot at soydev.link slash dn simple.

46
00:03:20,560 --> 00:03:29,860
In order to answer the question, why are we moving off of next? We first have to answer a more interesting question Why did we use it in the first place?

47
00:03:30,702 --> 00:03:37,956
And this question is actually, in my opinion, more interesting and more complex than the bigger one of why we moved off.

48
00:03:38,478 --> 00:03:45,888
When I was building T3 chat, I had a couple specific goals in mind. I wanted it to be as fast as possible as an application you would use.

49
00:03:45,968 --> 00:03:49,728
Like you click things, they happen immediately, you send a message, you get a response as quickly as possible.

50
00:03:49,968 --> 00:03:56,036
We really wanted it to feel and be as fast as reasonably achievable by us as a small team.

51
00:03:56,430 --> 00:04:03,492
I also wanted it to be scalable in more senses than just it will deploy across as many servers as we need it to for as many users as we have.

52
00:04:03,790 --> 00:04:10,448
I also wanted the code base itself to be written in a scalable way where we could add new functionality, we could extend portions, we could add features,

53
00:04:10,608 --> 00:04:16,420
we could keep building on it without feeling like we're drowning under the weight of what we had built into.

54
00:04:16,718 --> 00:04:25,808
I also didn't want to waste a ton of time trying to build the relationship between the server side and client side, especially because I knew we would be changing the server side a lot throughout.

55
00:04:25,998 --> 00:04:33,968
So I wanted to make sure everything would stay in sync relatively well because our client side logic initially was all about syncing our like data,

56
00:04:34,048 --> 00:04:41,408
because I put so much effort into our custom sync engine that if the back end and front end ever disagreed, you'd be at risk of losing all of your data.

57
00:04:41,806 --> 00:04:47,248
So the key pieces that we wanted were scalable design, server client in sync.

58
00:04:47,758 --> 00:04:53,700
And when I say in sync, I don't mean our CDN deploys within 30 seconds to two minutes of our servers changing.

59
00:04:54,094 --> 00:05:03,204
I mean it's really hard for those things to be out of sync. That whenever you're on our site and you do a thing, the back end and front end are the same version and understand each other.

60
00:05:03,502 --> 00:05:09,568
Alongside that, I wanted an easy way to deploy TS in sync specifically.

61
00:05:09,774 --> 00:05:16,368
I wanted to make sure the server and client were all TypeScript, everything was agreed from the front to the back, and it was easy for us to keep those parts attached.

62
00:05:16,750 --> 00:05:17,892
Obviously, I wanted React.

63
00:05:18,542 --> 00:05:19,044
Duh.

64
00:05:19,438 --> 00:05:23,428
I didn't want server components though, because I wanted everything to be client-side focused.

65
00:05:24,014 --> 00:05:25,252
And most importantly,

66
00:05:26,062 --> 00:05:34,528
I wanted to bet on boring tech. I know this doesn't seem very much like me, but the core library that powered most of T3

67
00:05:34,688 --> 00:05:38,768
chat in its initial version was a beautiful library.

68
00:05:38,990 --> 00:05:41,060
I still miss all of the time.

69
00:05:41,390 --> 00:05:48,048
We were building on top of IndexedDB, which is a browser standard for storing more data than you can store in local storage.

70
00:05:48,334 --> 00:05:56,290
It was meant to replace WebSQL, which sucks because WebSQL is a much better standard, and IndexedDB is it's a standard, roughly, but index DB

71
00:05:56,654 --> 00:06:04,964
is so unreliable, especially across different browsers, that back in the IE7 days, Dexy was created in order to

72
00:06:05,390 --> 00:06:06,724
make it all viable. Dexy

73
00:06:07,968 --> 00:06:12,676
is ancient, even if the website doesn't look like it. It's actually we have never seen the website in this state.

74
00:06:13,248 --> 00:06:14,980
They redid it recently.

75
00:06:15,918 --> 00:06:19,940
And if we go back to when I adopted it, which would have been in January of this year,

76
00:06:22,766 --> 00:06:23,812
this is what it looked like.

77
00:06:25,838 --> 00:06:27,204
And you can kind of tell

78
00:06:28,910 --> 00:06:29,568
it's older.

79
00:06:29,934 --> 00:06:32,688
That doesn't mean it's bad, it just means it's old.

80
00:06:32,768 --> 00:06:39,556
And in fact, its age was one of the factors that made me really excited to use it because it was super reliable.

81
00:06:40,078 --> 00:06:47,780
The only issues I had with Dexy were things that lived underneath it because indexed DB is a shit standard.

82
00:06:48,288 --> 00:06:51,888
I really enjoyed using Dexy. This is the library that powered everything.

83
00:06:52,110 --> 00:06:54,608
It was boring, it was old, it was great.

84
00:06:54,926 --> 00:06:59,488
All of that said, building my own sync engine was rough. So that ended up being one of the first things we removed.

85
00:06:59,726 --> 00:07:02,276
Not Dexy, but the whole concept of us running our own sync.

86
00:07:02,766 --> 00:07:07,396
But I was really thinking about all of this when I made the choices I made for T3 chat.

87
00:07:07,790 --> 00:07:09,764
So, how does Next play into these?

88
00:07:10,254 --> 00:07:18,180
Well, believe it or not, there are a lot of companies that are deploying gigantic Next.js apps without too many issues, especially now with TurboPack finally happening.

89
00:07:18,606 --> 00:07:26,404
On top of that, the Next.js way of building and deploying, especially when you use products like Vercel, Netlify, or Cloudflare, tends to scale very well.

90
00:07:26,830 --> 00:07:28,496
So it was a pretty easy choice for that.

91
00:07:28,942 --> 00:07:35,056
Server client in sync, this is one of the biggest things that Next.js gave us, alongside things like SKU protection on Vercel,

92
00:07:35,296 --> 00:07:38,308
where you can make sure the front and the back end are the same at all times.

93
00:07:38,670 --> 00:07:45,136
And our little hacks we did to let you know on client that your version's out of date and you have to refresh made it really easy to keep things in sync.

94
00:07:45,358 --> 00:07:52,576
What I actually wanted to deploy though, if we were to host the servers one place and the client another, keeping those in sync in time and making the code base

95
00:07:52,656 --> 00:07:56,932
easy to work with between the two was non-trivial, unless we used a framework like Next.

96
00:07:57,262 --> 00:08:05,136
Even though the backend and front end were very much separate, having them in the same project in the same package JSON made it way easier to deploy, revert,

97
00:08:05,358 --> 00:08:06,980
fall back, and keep everything in sync.

98
00:08:07,406 --> 00:08:12,452
I have done the thing where you split your backend and front end up, even though they could theoretically be together.

99
00:08:12,718 --> 00:08:15,936
And it just makes a lot of random shit way, way harder.

100
00:08:16,270 --> 00:08:21,796
Like the amount of times we had to freeze an old deployment or roll back as a result of a migration like this.

101
00:08:22,286 --> 00:08:31,844
If you have to go do that in multiple places and hope to keep things in sync, especially if you have a local DB that people are using that you did a migration on, good luck migrating it backwards.

102
00:08:32,366 --> 00:08:35,056
It seems like there's some confusion about keeping the front end and back end in sync.

103
00:08:35,136 --> 00:08:36,016
I'll make this simple.

104
00:08:36,206 --> 00:08:38,256
Let's say you have a front end and a backend.

105
00:08:38,766 --> 00:08:40,004
You have a version for each.

106
00:08:40,334 --> 00:08:44,164
You are on version 1.0.0 for the front end and for the backend.

107
00:08:44,686 --> 00:08:50,336
Now you make a change, and that change affects the signature of data. So you do a minor bump.

108
00:08:50,416 --> 00:08:51,844
You make it 1.1.0.

109
00:08:52,270 --> 00:08:56,676
In sync means that a user on the old version isn't hitting the new version.

110
00:08:57,006 --> 00:09:04,260
In sync means if I have to revert the back end deployment because something went wrong, that a user on the new version isn't hitting the old version.

111
00:09:04,846 --> 00:09:12,496
In sync effectively means in an ideal world, these things are compartmentalized and boxed and tied tightly together.

112
00:09:12,846 --> 00:09:15,376
If your front end's on version 1.0.0, your back end is.

113
00:09:15,456 --> 00:09:17,764
If your front end's on 1.1, your backend is.

114
00:09:18,416 --> 00:09:25,636
You want these to stay in sync because the amount of bugs that occur when your front end and back end are out of sync is absurd.

115
00:09:26,094 --> 00:09:28,416
And it is a lot easier if they are served together.

116
00:09:28,814 --> 00:09:36,976
Hopefully you can now understand why these two things are very important to me. I want to have a thing running on my machine, see everything is fine, hit deploy, and have everything still be fine.

117
00:09:37,166 --> 00:09:42,020
Especially if we have to worry about regressions, migrations, reverts, and all of those types of things.

118
00:09:42,350 --> 00:09:44,356
Having stuff in sync makes life much easier.

119
00:09:44,736 --> 00:09:45,188
Period. We

120
00:09:45,856 --> 00:09:48,932
also wanted to use React. I should not have to justify that one.

121
00:09:49,358 --> 00:09:51,056
We use React, our whole team knows React.

122
00:09:51,182 --> 00:09:53,936
The tech that we're building around is all built in and for React.

123
00:09:54,030 --> 00:09:55,216
It is a good ecosystem.

124
00:09:55,406 --> 00:09:56,452
The performance is fine.

125
00:09:56,736 --> 00:10:00,132
The worst performing AI chat up I ever used was written in Svelte.

126
00:10:00,576 --> 00:10:01,536
So yeah.

127
00:10:01,966 --> 00:10:04,096
Framework is not what makes your site slow.

128
00:10:04,256 --> 00:10:12,100
If your framework is actually what's making your site slow, you're either God to your dev and shouldn't be talking about it, or you're a terrible dev and also shouldn't be talking about it.

129
00:10:12,686 --> 00:10:15,364
And then this bottom piece here, I wanted to bet on boring tech.

130
00:10:16,046 --> 00:10:22,820
Whether or not you love Next.js or all the cool things that have shipped in it, if you don't use any of those new things, Next is very boring.

131
00:10:23,214 --> 00:10:25,056
And we were not using any of those new things.

132
00:10:25,296 --> 00:10:25,924
In fact,

133
00:10:26,574 --> 00:10:31,748
one of the better but also worse decisions I made was how we abuse the Next.js router.

134
00:10:32,174 --> 00:10:41,216
Since the Next router kind of assumes server and client, where when you navigate to a page, you might have to load the page from the server, trigger some other functions,

135
00:10:41,376 --> 00:10:42,756
and then get it on client.

136
00:10:43,150 --> 00:10:49,696
The type of snappy you click in your there navigation that I was aiming for wasn't super viable with the Next.js router.

137
00:10:50,030 --> 00:10:57,956
The NextJS router was super focused on server render, client render hybrid apps, or full server rendered apps, not on client focused apps.

138
00:10:58,350 --> 00:10:59,776
I really wanted our app to fly.

139
00:10:59,936 --> 00:11:03,012
Once you load the JS, you click a thing, you're there immediately.

140
00:11:03,534 --> 00:11:05,540
So our stack ended up being interesting.

141
00:11:06,414 --> 00:11:08,324
Our stack at launch was Next.js,

142
00:11:09,102 --> 00:11:10,948
but it also wasn't using the next router.

143
00:11:11,406 --> 00:11:13,092
I hacked in React router.

144
00:11:13,646 --> 00:11:24,100
I did some nasty things to make that work. In particular, I set up a rewrite rule that would redirect all URLs that weren't bound to go to the index HTML effective file

145
00:11:24,590 --> 00:11:34,144
because that would allow you to reroute the traffic. And if you go to a URL that doesn't exist, because it doesn't exist in the project, like let me switch to an old version of D3 chat code base.

146
00:11:34,304 --> 00:11:35,396
Give me one sec to do that.

147
00:11:35,904 --> 00:11:41,184
Here is all of the routes that we had in our app back when it was still on Next.js.

148
00:11:41,710 --> 00:11:46,212
If you've seen the source app directory in a Next project, it's probably full of a lot of different things.

149
00:11:46,574 --> 00:11:47,204
Ours isn't.

150
00:11:47,470 --> 00:11:51,424
We have API, which has a couple API endpoints because that's again what we were using Next for.

151
00:11:51,504 --> 00:12:00,420
We were effectively using it as an easy way to bundle a React client-side app and a TypeScript server-side app together so they would deploy together, be served together, and stay in sync.

152
00:12:00,686 --> 00:12:09,316
So we have our APIs for things like TRPC upload thing, status server version, and some internal stuff that we were using for migrations for data. That's all been deprecated long ago.

153
00:12:09,646 --> 00:12:12,624
And then we had our chat endpoint, which was our main chat.

154
00:12:12,846 --> 00:12:16,864
This is what would serve the request that will actually generate the text for your app.

155
00:12:17,230 --> 00:12:19,940
We also have this max duration thing, which I'm sure we'll get to in a little bit.

156
00:12:20,398 --> 00:12:20,900
Fun hack.

157
00:12:21,134 --> 00:12:22,788
But the pages was pretty boring.

158
00:12:23,310 --> 00:12:24,452
We have the status page.

159
00:12:24,814 --> 00:12:28,932
We have our docs pages for like terms of service, privacy policy, FAQ, security, all that.

160
00:12:29,294 --> 00:12:30,756
And then we had the app shell.

161
00:12:31,104 --> 00:12:33,380
This is where all of our app was served from.

162
00:12:34,062 --> 00:12:35,492
Yes, the whole thing.

163
00:12:35,886 --> 00:12:38,532
This is the cursed file that defines the entire client-side app.

164
00:12:38,766 --> 00:12:41,796
I'm using React Router because I want a great client first experience.

165
00:12:42,144 --> 00:12:44,068
This commit was 11 months ago.

166
00:12:45,102 --> 00:12:45,732
Beautiful.

167
00:12:46,414 --> 00:12:54,384
We also had this four statics, just brute forcing Next.js to always render this as a static page and not server render it ever, as well as this SSR false,

168
00:12:54,624 --> 00:12:58,756
which specifically tells Next during both build and when serving this,

169
00:12:59,504 --> 00:13:01,904
do not render this on this page.

170
00:13:02,094 --> 00:13:05,380
Do not mount this and render whatever HTML comes out.

171
00:13:05,902 --> 00:13:09,264
Just render an empty div and we'll put this in at client later.

172
00:13:09,518 --> 00:13:14,544
This file was how I brute forced Next.js to allow me to use React Router, but that's not all.

173
00:13:14,784 --> 00:13:17,508
Because now we have this static app shell route.

174
00:13:17,774 --> 00:13:23,844
But what about the main route? What about like the core page? What about index? What about the chat routes?

175
00:13:24,206 --> 00:13:26,864
This doesn't handle the other routes. In fact, it doesn't handle any route.

176
00:13:27,086 --> 00:13:29,124
And nobody's going to slash static app shell on our app.

177
00:13:29,710 --> 00:13:31,424
So how did we handle all of that?

178
00:13:31,822 --> 00:13:35,524
Here's where we get into my more hardcore abuse of next.

179
00:13:36,014 --> 00:13:38,464
We hop into the rewrite section here.

180
00:13:38,830 --> 00:13:42,020
Routes that aren't in our file-based routing should be handled by React Router.

181
00:13:42,144 --> 00:13:44,804
This is the only way to make all of those routes static.

182
00:13:45,198 --> 00:13:53,824
Because if we were to dynamically reroute traffic based on what route you're on, that would become server-side. And now we're not getting those client-side benefits I'm aiming for.

183
00:13:54,094 --> 00:14:03,024
So we wanted to make sure all traffic that doesn't go to an existing URL, because like if it goes to slash API slash event, or if it goes to slash API slash chat,

184
00:14:03,104 --> 00:14:05,476
we want it to be rendered with other things.

185
00:14:05,934 --> 00:14:12,612
But we also want to make sure all fallback URLs, so like the 404s and everything, go to our static app shell.

186
00:14:12,942 --> 00:14:22,852
So here is the hack we have this app shell redirect where all sources will be sent to the static app shell unless they have this XTRPC key.

187
00:14:22,958 --> 00:14:25,924
Because TRPC also has a glob pattern.

188
00:14:26,384 --> 00:14:28,484
I tried putting in here originally, didn't work.

189
00:14:28,750 --> 00:14:36,704
Since TRPC's endpoint can have additional like sub paths, it was impossible for us to get that to work without using custom headers

190
00:14:36,704 --> 00:14:44,068
in order to identify which traffic was supposed to be handled by TRPC because otherwise the TRPC traffic would be routed to static app shell and wouldn't work.

191
00:14:44,494 --> 00:14:45,024
Not fun.

192
00:14:45,454 --> 00:14:46,784
See, static means static.

193
00:14:47,022 --> 00:14:51,172
And then this was 10 months ago we moved to TRPC, fixed TRPC queries getting eaten by the rewrites.

194
00:14:51,790 --> 00:14:52,132
Yeah.

195
00:14:52,544 --> 00:14:59,024
I cannot properly explain how many random bullshit things were caused by this.

196
00:14:59,630 --> 00:15:02,544
On one hand, it was less than expected. I'm surprised this worked at all.

197
00:15:02,704 --> 00:15:05,584
On the other hand, this was hellish and nobody should do it.

198
00:15:05,902 --> 00:15:11,744
If the Next.js router is not the right router for your app, the NextJS is not the right framework for your app, move out of it.

199
00:15:11,950 --> 00:15:18,852
As soon as I added this to the code base, I understood that Next.js was not the long term solution for T3 chat.

200
00:15:19,118 --> 00:15:22,916
It was a temp solution until we had a better path that made more sense to us.

201
00:15:23,406 --> 00:15:26,404
At that exact time, I started exploring other options.

202
00:15:26,990 --> 00:15:30,624
So your next React Router, indexDB with Dexie.

203
00:15:31,342 --> 00:15:35,972
Redis as our DB, mostly just to back up whatever we were doing.

204
00:15:36,560 --> 00:15:39,972
And Vercel with Fluid for our runtime.

205
00:15:40,238 --> 00:15:45,988
I don't feel like going really deep on Fluid Compute right now. Go watch any of my other videos about it if you want to know more.

206
00:15:46,542 --> 00:15:54,160
By March, we were hitting the limits of how I could abuse Redis for this, so we moved this out and replaced it with Planet Scale and left pretty much everything else the same.

207
00:15:54,574 --> 00:15:56,484
Then in May and June, we did the convex move.

208
00:15:56,750 --> 00:16:00,240
In July, Julia started working on the effect rewrite for the back end.

209
00:16:00,622 --> 00:16:11,920
But this whole time, I was thinking in the back of my head, we put ourselves in a weird place using next for this. Around the same time in March, I was exploring what a rewrite would look like here.

210
00:16:12,142 --> 00:16:15,396
Also, Vercel hadn't really put up Fluid yet, so I should remove that.

211
00:16:15,680 --> 00:16:21,040
I was looking at how crazy our inference time was because we were doing really long requests for these generations.

212
00:16:21,262 --> 00:16:27,876
And the way Vercel was scaling that with their existing compute methods was not great because every user got a dedicated lambda for every chat.

213
00:16:28,142 --> 00:16:35,876
Fluid made that a lot cheaper, but before Fluid dropped, I wanted to make sure we had an exit plan that we can move to Cloudflare if needed, which should be way cheaper,

214
00:16:36,110 --> 00:16:43,556
and that we didn't need to be so reliant on Next, because Next wasn't really benefiting us beyond being an easy way to deploy our TypeScript backend and front end together.

215
00:16:43,726 --> 00:16:48,900
and alongside that was causing us problems like these hacks I was doing to force, React Router to work.

216
00:16:49,294 --> 00:16:52,196
Since we were on React Router, I wanted to stick with React Router.

217
00:16:52,334 --> 00:16:56,580
So my next attempt was Remix, which, if I'm being honest, kind of failed.

218
00:16:57,070 --> 00:17:05,540
There were a lot of weird things with how Remix deploys on different platforms that made it kind of hard for us to get it working properly on Cloudflare.

219
00:17:05,774 --> 00:17:09,444
Around the same time, React Router server version became more of a thing.

220
00:17:10,030 --> 00:17:16,480
So the React Router server friendly edition was something I did explore a lot more and got pretty far with that as well.

221
00:17:16,814 --> 00:17:18,720
But I also didn't trust some of the primitives.

222
00:17:18,800 --> 00:17:28,160
They hadn't really figured out what they wanted to have for like posts and gets and all of these different solutions for fetching data from a server, defining endpoints, etc.

223
00:17:28,686 --> 00:17:34,080
It wasn't quite standard. It was dancing around the standards, and it was really, really poorly documented at the time.

224
00:17:34,414 --> 00:17:38,852
And I specifically didn't want to bet on an under-documented, under-explored technology.

225
00:17:39,438 --> 00:17:45,280
So the bet I was making was that a VET plus Hano app would be our path forward.

226
00:17:45,518 --> 00:17:51,040
I ended up independently in March and April rewriting all of T3 chat to be a VT HANO

227
00:17:51,040 --> 00:17:54,240
app and went through hell getting it to deploy on Cloudflare.

228
00:17:54,798 --> 00:18:00,004
This was around the time that Cloudflare was trying to kill pages and move it into part of workers.

229
00:18:00,654 --> 00:18:09,040
And man, talk about under documented just trying to find a way to have it so the slash API endpoint would be served by Hano

230
00:18:09,440 --> 00:18:21,440
and everything else would be served by the static V bundle required me to reverse engineer shit. I was deep in like output bundles that were generated by worker D and by um fucking what's it called?

231
00:18:21,520 --> 00:18:27,460
Wrangler to try and figure out how assets were being tagged so I could manually redirect traffic to the right place.

232
00:18:27,854 --> 00:18:28,960
It was miserable.

233
00:18:29,230 --> 00:18:35,520
Trying to get Cloudflare to point to the right traffic at the right place through a single worker definition was so hellish that when I complained,

234
00:18:35,600 --> 00:18:42,160
they ended up going out and putting out a bunch of reference material on how to do it right, as well as public repos that, as far as I know, they do actually maintain now.

235
00:18:42,350 --> 00:18:50,340
But also, as far as I know, I was one of the first people to get Vlushano for static deployment for your assets, dynamic deployment for your endpoints

236
00:18:50,880 --> 00:18:52,640
working on Cloudflare ever.

237
00:18:52,942 --> 00:18:53,760
So I am proud of that.

238
00:18:54,062 --> 00:19:01,920
I know I'm the Versailles guy, but I put my time in on Cloudflare and ultimately decided against it because the point of these moves was to get

239
00:19:01,920 --> 00:19:04,868
away from these types of hacks and these underdocumented things.

240
00:19:05,134 --> 00:19:16,800
And believe it or not, my React router in next thing felt less uncertain than all these other paths that I explored because all of these paths were super underexplored by others.

241
00:19:16,974 --> 00:19:24,292
And I really did not want to give up my synchronization piece where the front end and back end deploy together and stay in sync together.

242
00:19:25,040 --> 00:19:25,360
So

243
00:19:26,286 --> 00:19:29,840
yeah, I didn't like the uncharted territory I was getting into.

244
00:19:30,158 --> 00:19:35,556
And then I tried moving a few more things over, and we hit the bundle size limits for Cloudflare functions.

245
00:19:35,854 --> 00:19:44,080
Apparently, it's a very common problem in bigger server side rendered frameworks when you're building on Cloudflare. It is very easy to hit limits on how big the bundles can be.

246
00:19:44,320 --> 00:19:47,588
Some of our routes are like 20 megs plus in size.

247
00:19:48,238 --> 00:19:49,796
And I think Cloudflare's limits two.

248
00:19:50,414 --> 00:19:55,332
Yeah, the worker size is limited to three megs for the free tier and 10 megs for the paid tier.

249
00:19:56,622 --> 00:20:01,600
Static assets can be much bigger. So the actual assets that you're serving, using, screwing with is fine.

250
00:20:01,870 --> 00:20:06,340
But the JS that executes has a relatively low cap in how big it can get.

251
00:20:06,830 --> 00:20:13,124
And these caps are easy to hit with the type of stuff that we're doing, especially once we started doing like image compression on the server side and stuff.

252
00:20:14,158 --> 00:20:18,084
Even if Cloudflare supported proper node with native bindings, we would hit these limits.

253
00:20:18,510 --> 00:20:21,828
People are asking why serve the static assets from the worker and not the CDN.

254
00:20:22,286 --> 00:20:27,556
Not that simple. We were serving static assets from the CDN, but our problem wasn't the static assets.

255
00:20:27,680 --> 00:20:31,684
Our problem was the server side code and the packages we were using on the server side.

256
00:20:32,078 --> 00:20:35,840
So it was relatively easy for us to hit this. Any big app is going to hit this eventually.

257
00:20:36,046 --> 00:20:38,628
And we hit it as soon as we started moving over.

258
00:20:38,958 --> 00:20:42,916
So once again, decided it's not worth the fighting here to get all of this working.

259
00:20:43,438 --> 00:20:51,460
I would have to make our deployment system way more complex and create many different workers for many different routes and find some way to manage this all at a higher level.

260
00:20:51,758 --> 00:20:58,916
Not only would the front end and back end not necessarily be in sync, the front end and various different endpoints on the back end would no longer necessarily be in sync.

261
00:20:59,246 --> 00:21:04,880
I did look at things like Porter and CloudFront and again concluded more work, little gain.

262
00:21:05,198 --> 00:21:16,740
And on top of that, we'd either have to run our own dedicated servers and manage breaking traffic across between them during generations, or build our own equivalent of fluid compute on Lambda,

263
00:21:17,102 --> 00:21:20,932
or we can just use Vercel and have the fluid compute on Lambda benefits, which is what we wanted to do.

264
00:21:21,422 --> 00:21:28,420
Again, around this time was when Fluid happened, and it made it a lot harder to justify moving to something like Cloudflare or our own servers.

265
00:21:29,134 --> 00:21:33,988
Fluid is really good for these types of things. Watch any of my videos about it if you don't understand.

266
00:21:34,478 --> 00:21:38,596
Really like this message from chat. Unless you have dedicated system admins, Vercel sounds like a good choice.

267
00:21:38,926 --> 00:21:39,268
Yes.

268
00:21:39,662 --> 00:21:42,720
At the time, we had two employees, me and Mark.

269
00:21:42,958 --> 00:21:45,028
And I'm barely an employee. I have so much other shit to do.

270
00:21:45,486 --> 00:21:50,660
Julius was part-time, still finishing up school, but we got him to move here full-time, which was a huge help as well.

271
00:21:50,990 --> 00:22:00,548
But our options were limited because we did not have dedicated people running our infrastructure for us. And even if we did, that would slow down our inertia meaningfully.

272
00:22:01,742 --> 00:22:03,680
But these were not the only options I explored.

273
00:22:03,950 --> 00:22:06,532
I did actually start exploring Tanstack Start at this time.

274
00:22:06,798 --> 00:22:10,800
Funny enough, the Tanstack Start port wasn't really started by me.

275
00:22:11,150 --> 00:22:14,276
Here was the first attempt at the Tanstack Start PR for T3 chat.

276
00:22:14,990 --> 00:22:16,320
Oh shit, I was around the timelines.

277
00:22:16,400 --> 00:22:17,360
This was in February.

278
00:22:17,550 --> 00:22:19,908
I was exploring other options that early.

279
00:22:20,526 --> 00:22:22,980
This one was opened by Tanner himself.

280
00:22:23,520 --> 00:22:28,388
He started from my attempt to get React Router working, which you can see here.

281
00:22:28,718 --> 00:22:30,948
I was very pumped I finally got it working.

282
00:22:31,982 --> 00:22:35,588
And he used that as a starting point to start moving us over to Tannstack.

283
00:22:35,680 --> 00:22:44,240
He got pretty far, but concluded that the single-page app stuff wasn't quite where we would want it yet, without even asking. He's just like, I want to get this better first.

284
00:22:44,320 --> 00:22:53,188
We can talk about this later, and went off and kept improving things on his side. And Tanstack Start kept getting better and better until it got where it is today, which is honestly incredible.

285
00:22:53,486 --> 00:23:02,628
This PR ultimately died because the code base moved way too fast and it was just an experiment. Like he was building an experimental PR on top of my experimental PR.

286
00:23:02,926 --> 00:23:11,076
So anybody saying that we moved right now because of some exploit or whatever, no, we've been exploring this since February, and we went live in January.

287
00:23:11,440 --> 00:23:21,200
For the vast majority of our time working on T3 chat, we have been exploring other options to figure out how we get out of the next JS Plus React router

288
00:23:21,360 --> 00:23:23,600
mess that I used to get us to our first launch.

289
00:23:23,886 --> 00:23:32,160
And even at this point, it felt like Tanzdax Start was the closest to what we were looking for. It had good concepts of the primitives between the back end and the front end.

290
00:23:32,366 --> 00:23:37,840
It had routing that made sense, that gave us the nice file system stuff that I'm 50-50 on.

291
00:23:38,000 --> 00:23:43,492
In a lot of ways, I miss traditional routing in a like JavaScript file where you define the routes yourself.

292
00:23:43,822 --> 00:23:51,876
But in other ways, file-based routing is really, really nice and useful. It makes things way clearer when you're trying to just find the place that the thing's being rendered from.

293
00:23:52,206 --> 00:23:54,372
Also makes bundle splitting significantly easier too.

294
00:23:54,862 --> 00:23:57,600
Tanstack Start had the right combo of things.

295
00:23:57,934 --> 00:24:04,676
And when you go back to what we wanted, which was an easy way to deploy our front end and our back end together with good primitives that stay in sync,

296
00:24:05,102 --> 00:24:10,980
Tanstack Start is a heavy solution, to be clear, because there's a lot of things in there that we weren't necessarily interested in.

297
00:24:11,310 --> 00:24:19,600
But it was also a complete solution built by somebody working really hard on rethinking things from the ground up that wasn't full of all of the tech debt

298
00:24:19,760 --> 00:24:23,588
and years of legacy things to maintain compatibility with that Next.js is.

299
00:24:24,014 --> 00:24:27,120
On that note, I do need to glaze next a little bit.

300
00:24:27,598 --> 00:24:34,756
I don't think we take enough time to appreciate how incredible it is that Next.js is as backwards compatible as it is.

301
00:24:35,022 --> 00:24:40,900
You could upgrade a Next 10 app to Next 16 and have almost no issues.

302
00:24:41,262 --> 00:24:46,564
And the few you might have, they write code mods for that you can execute and do that port with.

303
00:24:46,830 --> 00:24:53,828
It is a little bit absurd how good the backcompat is with both Next.js and with React as a whole.

304
00:24:54,208 --> 00:25:00,196
And the work that the Next team does to maintain that backwards compatibility is incredible and they should be commended for it.

305
00:25:00,494 --> 00:25:06,852
They work really, really hard and they build a stable framework that I do not regret betting on for every other project I've built.

306
00:25:07,182 --> 00:25:16,164
They continue to improve it, they continue to maintain it, they continue to give us paths to make our code bases, make our user experiences, and make our lives better by building on top of Next.

307
00:25:16,528 --> 00:25:23,808
In fact, I honestly believe if we waited a few more months and I had pushed the next team a little bit harder on the client side SPA stuff,

308
00:25:24,078 --> 00:25:28,100
we would have gotten to a point where I could have removed React Router, removed a bunch of these hacks, and been happy.

309
00:25:28,558 --> 00:25:30,980
But I also really like Tanner and wanted a fresh start.

310
00:25:31,598 --> 00:25:39,728
I was briefly considering building my own framework at this point in time, taking minimal pieces from Hano and Fastify, combining that with Veet for rendering

311
00:25:39,888 --> 00:25:42,948
the client-side stuff, and trying to build this

312
00:25:43,694 --> 00:25:52,164
better, simple, minimal client-side app with server-side TypeScript bound in a way that deploys together thing. And Tanstext Start does that all for me.

313
00:25:52,590 --> 00:25:57,808
we evaluated all these options, got distracted with problems that didn't exist here. Like our app is layers.

314
00:25:57,888 --> 00:26:04,996
We have the infrastructure, we have the server side code, the client side code, the components themselves, the data, all of these pieces.

315
00:26:05,488 --> 00:26:14,208
The problems that we were encountering at this point were lots of things breaking in my sync engine because I built my own way to keep data in sync, and it was not good.

316
00:26:14,414 --> 00:26:15,972
It was actually quite bad.

317
00:26:16,528 --> 00:26:20,612
The yeah, the amount of issues from the sync engine were crazy.

318
00:26:21,038 --> 00:26:25,168
Switching to TanStack or V or whatever else would not have fixed any of those problems.

319
00:26:25,454 --> 00:26:32,928
So that was what I know life. And I spent all of May and a good chunk of June rewriting all of our data layer, sync engine, and everything, with Convex.

320
00:26:33,166 --> 00:26:36,768
That video is already live if you want to deep dive on what it looks like to do this type of migration.

321
00:26:37,006 --> 00:26:37,728
It was hellish.

322
00:26:37,888 --> 00:26:47,524
We pulled it off. Then my custom auth layer that was on a forked and abused version of openoth was having some problems, and we really wanted to start adding new auth providers.

323
00:26:47,822 --> 00:26:50,208
So the next move was to switch to an auth provider.

324
00:26:50,288 --> 00:26:59,140
We went with WorkOS, questionable migration overall for the better, and enables a lot of things that we're excited about, but it was a non-trivial migration to say the least.

325
00:26:59,502 --> 00:27:05,444
And then finally, we went and did the effect rewrite on the back end because we wanted better observability.

326
00:27:05,966 --> 00:27:14,340
All of those things reduced a significant portion of the bugs that users were experiencing and the issues that we were having trying to debug the things users were seeing.

327
00:27:14,830 --> 00:27:20,484
But what was left, once we had our traces from effect on the server, once we had a better sync engine all through Convex,

328
00:27:20,878 --> 00:27:28,740
the bugs that were left were largely around the weird relationship between our React router client side app and our Next.js server side app

329
00:27:29,198 --> 00:27:31,524
that were not ever built to work together well.

330
00:27:31,918 --> 00:27:37,604
So without even really asking me, Julius just started the Tan Stack rewrite.

331
00:27:38,126 --> 00:27:39,364
And I want to be very clear about this.

332
00:27:40,398 --> 00:27:46,308
Julius didn't take all the research and effort I put in, ask me what the best option was and go with it.

333
00:27:46,958 --> 00:27:48,928
I didn't hire Julius to listen to me.

334
00:27:49,358 --> 00:27:53,028
I hired Julius to make good changes to our code base and maintain things for us.

335
00:27:53,550 --> 00:27:57,220
I hired him to be a good developer pushing the quality of the project and the app forward.

336
00:27:57,806 --> 00:28:01,284
And he independently chose Tanstack Start as the correct solution.

337
00:28:01,774 --> 00:28:03,876
And I almost entirely agree.

338
00:28:04,430 --> 00:28:12,708
There are parts of me that wish we more deeply explored the VT Plus HANA or a VT Plus Fastify solution because I really do want this to be a client side, client side app.

339
00:28:13,998 --> 00:28:18,724
But Tan Stack Start does have a lot to offer us, and I am happy with the move.

340
00:28:19,086 --> 00:28:21,572
Which I guess leads us to talking about said move.

341
00:28:21,998 --> 00:28:22,980
Here's the PR.

342
00:28:23,278 --> 00:28:29,540
14,000 lines added, 10,000 removed. doesn't even include the patch we had to do after in order to get it like actually working reliably.

343
00:28:29,710 --> 00:28:34,032
But this was the core main rewrite that we shipped to beta so that we and a few others could test it.

344
00:28:34,318 --> 00:28:35,172
Fix a bunch of bugs.

345
00:28:35,662 --> 00:28:39,140
And do a large number of changes in the pull request, only one file is being shown at a time.

346
00:28:39,952 --> 00:28:41,284
Let me go to graphite.

347
00:28:41,998 --> 00:28:53,392
Unintentional graphite add their GitHub review view, because they're a GitHub wrapper, makes these giant PRs actually reviewable, which is essential when you're doing these types of huge migrations.

348
00:28:53,472 --> 00:28:54,628
GitHub won't even open them.

349
00:28:55,406 --> 00:29:02,468
So here we have that actual PR. Thank you for actually working Graphite, unlike GitHub, for looking at something like this.

350
00:29:02,926 --> 00:29:08,068
And we have our pile of patches that made certain things work because nothing works how we expect it to.

351
00:29:08,494 --> 00:29:12,228
But the more important parts here are how the new routing works.

352
00:29:12,686 --> 00:29:15,076
We got rid of my hacky app TSX.

353
00:29:15,566 --> 00:29:18,852
We got rid of that whole front-end folder, I believe, for the most part.

354
00:29:19,470 --> 00:29:21,316
Let me just pull the latest version here.

355
00:29:27,232 --> 00:29:27,552
One

356
00:29:27,598 --> 00:29:36,100
of the interesting things about Tanstack Start is the way it's a hybrid between generation for files and more traditional inference through TypeScript.

357
00:29:36,654 --> 00:29:44,512
You do have to have your route files defined in a specific way in order for the type safety to work, but it will automatically do that for you when the dev client is running.

358
00:29:44,592 --> 00:29:48,592
And if you change something that's incorrect, it will update it for you in the background.

359
00:29:48,910 --> 00:30:00,032
So when you create a new route, just the creation of the new route file will result in it making all of these other sub files for you, making sure that it exports the route here with the right name.

360
00:30:00,334 --> 00:30:07,236
And if I go to new, for example, and I change this to slash news, it will create a new route for that for me.

361
00:30:07,662 --> 00:30:11,396
Or here it just it uses the file name by default. So I change this to news.

362
00:30:11,662 --> 00:30:21,792
I'm saving now, and it got auto-rewritten by the dev server from Tanner stuff from TanStack in order to make sure that this is correct because TypeScript

363
00:30:21,952 --> 00:30:23,552
has no concept of what file you're in.

364
00:30:23,758 --> 00:30:27,632
So in order for the routing to be type safe, you have to pass the route you're on.

365
00:30:27,950 --> 00:30:33,796
So if you name this file differently, and this is where it gets really fun, if I rename this to news, see that the file got updated automatically.

366
00:30:34,126 --> 00:30:40,164
These are the things that Tanner had to do in order to make sure the full stack type safety worked as expected, and he succeeded.

367
00:30:40,782 --> 00:30:41,476
Pretty incredible.

368
00:30:41,952 --> 00:30:43,236
let's go back to our router.

369
00:30:43,758 --> 00:30:48,612
We have the getRouter function. We introduced the convex client so that we can hydrate it because

370
00:30:49,326 --> 00:30:55,620
Julius wanted to have the server side stuff work too, so that we could serve a render it and client render it with access to the same data.

371
00:30:56,110 --> 00:30:59,364
Also sets off on convex client based on your work OS auth state.

372
00:30:59,566 --> 00:31:06,272
Create a query client that will work on server and on client, connect it with the convex query client, make a tRPC client as well,

373
00:31:06,432 --> 00:31:13,476
because we still use TRPC for a bunch of random data on the server side, and then our create router function here, where we have our route tree, our scroll restoration,

374
00:31:13,872 --> 00:31:22,432
context for all the things that the router needs, and then the things that wrap it. These are like our providers for convex, for what is even the client runtime provider?

375
00:31:22,512 --> 00:31:23,712
Is that something from Tanner, I'm assuming?

376
00:31:24,014 --> 00:31:27,588
No, so manage runtime. That is for effect stuff, almost certainly.

377
00:31:28,078 --> 00:31:29,508
Yep, that's for effect stuff.

378
00:31:30,062 --> 00:31:35,940
And then the TRPC provider that allows our TRPC functions to work, and then props to children, which is where it will render everything else.

379
00:31:36,238 --> 00:31:42,592
Then we have our route tree. This file is generated, which is why it's.gen.ts and it has this comment at the top. This file should automatically be generated.

380
00:31:42,672 --> 00:31:44,708
You should not make changes to the files that will be overridden.

381
00:31:45,230 --> 00:31:45,668
Awesome.

382
00:31:46,446 --> 00:31:55,364
Again, that hybrid of code gen and actual files where the files in your code base. It's not a rewrite of things on your npm package like something like Prisma does.

383
00:31:55,758 --> 00:32:03,140
And a lot of the people who are building these big complex apps, like our friends over at Axiom, Gabriel and Crew, the way routes are declared is so good.

384
00:32:03,374 --> 00:32:03,940
I agree.

385
00:32:04,238 --> 00:32:10,020
One of the things that makes the way routes are declared so cool is how well it handles parameters being passed.

386
00:32:10,574 --> 00:32:15,428
So of course we have our chat endpoint, which is where the chat messages are.

387
00:32:15,790 --> 00:32:19,940
We also have chat share, chat index, and chat thread ID.

388
00:32:20,238 --> 00:32:23,044
Here's the important piece here chat dot thread ID.

389
00:32:23,312 --> 00:32:24,484
So what's going on here?

390
00:32:24,942 --> 00:32:25,712
We have our loader.

391
00:32:25,902 --> 00:32:36,580
The loader ensures that we have certain query data. In this case, it ensures that we have for the get threadby ID query the data for this specific thread ID.

392
00:32:36,974 --> 00:32:43,396
And the thread ID is defined as part of the params, which it gets typed safely through this name here.

393
00:32:43,854 --> 00:32:49,152
It knows that thread ID exists because it is inferring it off of the string that we defined here.

394
00:32:49,454 --> 00:32:54,992
And again, if I rename this to thread IDL, it'll rename that accordingly.

395
00:32:55,118 --> 00:32:59,364
And now I get a type error here because params does not have this specific field on it.

396
00:33:00,270 --> 00:33:07,524
If I rename it back, we're good with some type safety stuff because I'm using the new TypeScript Go thing, which doesn't recover from these things as well.

397
00:33:08,238 --> 00:33:09,700
Reset the language server and we're good to go.

398
00:33:10,190 --> 00:33:18,352
And then the actual route component, which is the thing that is returned here, which now knows that thread ID exists because it knows this route, which we defined here,

399
00:33:18,592 --> 00:33:19,472
has that on it.

400
00:33:19,758 --> 00:33:21,956
And it's again the magic of Tanstack stuff.

401
00:33:22,350 --> 00:33:28,804
The Tanstack router plus Tanstack start combo lets you define a route as a thing that resolves this URL.

402
00:33:29,152 --> 00:33:32,432
Has these it has this ID in the params.

403
00:33:32,992 --> 00:33:37,060
We have this data, we know it exists, and we have this component that gets rendered.

404
00:33:37,326 --> 00:33:45,152
Now we know for a fact, whenever we are calling a route component that is defined as this route, that the thread ID will exist.

405
00:33:45,392 --> 00:33:48,992
This convex query has been loaded, otherwise, this will not render.

406
00:33:49,166 --> 00:33:54,308
We could even return data here and know it'll be there if we call route.use data or something along those lines.

407
00:33:54,734 --> 00:34:05,156
We then render our background prefetcher to keep the other threads in sync, the chat, and render the message widget, well, a mock of it that we show when we don't have the rest of the data just yet.

408
00:34:05,614 --> 00:34:06,112
Awesome.

409
00:34:06,478 --> 00:34:14,948
But what about those backend functions? You know, the slash chat endpoints of the world that we relied on so heavily that was the reason that we were using next in the first place.

410
00:34:15,886 --> 00:34:20,772
as you see, our app folder is now empty. It just got left behind by a bad git pull.

411
00:34:21,038 --> 00:34:21,796
Happens a lot.

412
00:34:22,414 --> 00:34:25,028
We hop into backend slash API slash chat.

413
00:34:25,326 --> 00:34:31,300
You'll see we have this index.post.ts, this resume.get.ts, and abort postts.

414
00:34:32,942 --> 00:34:41,156
If you're deep in Tanstack stuff, you might be a little surprised because that's not the format that you usually use for exporting functions in Tanstack.

415
00:34:41,806 --> 00:34:45,604
You'll also see a lot of effect code here, which you might assume is the thing that we're doing,

416
00:34:46,734 --> 00:34:47,460
it is not.

417
00:34:48,110 --> 00:34:51,908
Here is where we start getting into the problems that we have with Tanstack.

418
00:34:52,142 --> 00:34:58,564
I don't know how many of these are Tanstack specific. I am not smart enough to go too deep into what caused these things because I did not do this port.

419
00:34:59,022 --> 00:35:00,804
We had a lot of problems trying to deploy this.

420
00:35:01,454 --> 00:35:03,012
The main one was this error.

421
00:35:03,438 --> 00:35:10,272
Error in root route, type error, fetch failed on the internal N DC fetch instance in Node itself.

422
00:35:10,382 --> 00:35:13,028
Yes, Node's fetch implementation is just in DC.

423
00:35:13,326 --> 00:35:22,404
We were constantly hitting this once we rolled out far enough. We'd hit about 60% rollout, and then this would start being seen in our servers, and clients weren't rendering.

424
00:35:22,830 --> 00:35:24,592
We had a lot of theories about this.

425
00:35:24,942 --> 00:35:30,564
I insisted that we turn off server rendering entirely because I didn't think we benefited from it, and this was terrifying.

426
00:35:31,054 --> 00:35:38,500
Apparently, a lot of people who have ported big apps over to Tanstack Start on Vercel have run into things like this. Gabriel said they've had the issue quite a bit for Axiom as well.

427
00:35:38,752 --> 00:35:42,596
We had it so bad that our deployments were crashing as soon as we hit like 60% rollout.

428
00:35:42,912 --> 00:35:43,632
It was not good.

429
00:35:43,792 --> 00:35:53,284
It was looking real, real bad. I bugged a lot of people at Vercel. I believe Julius and crew bug Tanner and a few other people working on the actual bundling side for Tanstack.

430
00:35:53,632 --> 00:35:54,372
It was rough.

431
00:35:54,926 --> 00:35:57,668
The solution ended up honestly being even rougher.

432
00:35:58,158 --> 00:36:10,084
The problem, is, I understand it, is that the way Tanstack was handling lazy bundling of routes was pulling in the JS for the route when the route was hit,

433
00:36:10,446 --> 00:36:16,900
but it would keep all of the JS there for all of the routes to be served. So every URL points to the same place.

434
00:36:17,422 --> 00:36:23,108
That place has as little JavaScript loaded as possible, but knows where the other bundles are.

435
00:36:23,342 --> 00:36:31,012
Once it sees the URL that you're requesting for, it would then go grab the right.js for that URL and then use that to serve the user.

436
00:36:31,392 --> 00:36:37,124
Now do that tens of thousands of times for every different route where different routes have different JS.

437
00:36:37,518 --> 00:36:41,956
And now add any bug to the cleanup logic or even a bug for how it dedupes.

438
00:36:42,158 --> 00:36:46,672
If five people make a request to the same endpoint at the same time and they're all served by this at the same time,

439
00:36:46,832 --> 00:36:51,172
I can see how easy it'd be to write the code that then goes and loads the same JS five times.

440
00:36:51,632 --> 00:37:01,348
We don't know the deep details just yet. Uh Puya and a few others are doing a call tomorrow to try and figure this out. I might even re-record this whole video once we get more info to be determined.

441
00:37:01,838 --> 00:37:11,716
The result was that we don't trust the Tan stack route resolvers for things other than like resolving the experience users are downloading HTML for.

442
00:37:12,078 --> 00:37:16,272
We could easily handle all the different routes people load and go to in the browser.

443
00:37:16,494 --> 00:37:22,340
But once we had all of these endpoints that we're trying to resolve on the same fluid compute instance at the same time,

444
00:37:22,702 --> 00:37:30,192
they would often hit problems where they were overloaded by pulling too many files in. We were getting this too many files error constantly.

445
00:37:30,478 --> 00:37:32,196
Yeah, the EM file code.

446
00:37:32,750 --> 00:37:33,472
It was rough.

447
00:37:34,222 --> 00:37:36,896
We tried a lot of different things to try and resolve this.

448
00:37:37,230 --> 00:37:43,780
After talking to Puya and a few others at Vercel, Puya suggested that we look more deeply into how Nitro handles routes.

449
00:37:44,096 --> 00:37:50,896
Nitro is an optional dependency for Tanstack, but it is a common one because it's how you deploy Tanstack on things like Vercel.

450
00:37:51,374 --> 00:37:55,776
Nitro is a set of tools for building server rendered toolkits primarily.

451
00:37:55,936 --> 00:38:03,524
If you want to make an app that works on server and on client and have one TypeScript code base that handles it all, Nitro is a set of primitives that make it possible.

452
00:38:03,886 --> 00:38:10,468
It was originally built as a deep part of the chaos that is the Nuxt ecosystem, you know, the Vue Nux world.

453
00:38:10,734 --> 00:38:19,108
But Nitro, along with the Nuxt team, got acquired by Vercel, and there's been a lot of work in standardizing Nitro so that it's way better, more reliable for giant apps.

454
00:38:19,566 --> 00:38:24,336
Ideally, you'll never have to know anything about Nitro because it lives a layer deeper than we should as application developers.

455
00:38:24,416 --> 00:38:30,736
But if you're trying to build your own alternative to Next.js or Nuxt or viewers felt kit or whatever, Nitro is a really important building block.

456
00:38:30,862 --> 00:38:36,336
And having Puya, who is the lead maintainer and creator of Nitro, helping us out has been essential.

457
00:38:36,526 --> 00:38:43,056
It was his belief that the way lazy loading works for routes in Nitro should handle all the problems that we were having.

458
00:38:43,342 --> 00:38:51,456
So Julius filed quite the pull request to run API routes as separate functions, which included patching the Tan Stack Start

459
00:38:51,616 --> 00:39:03,856
Server Core to export. this get H3 event binding, which is part of how Nitro handles events internally, so that we would have access to that, so we could bind our own nitro

460
00:39:04,016 --> 00:39:04,260
events.

461
00:39:04,496 --> 00:39:05,124
directly.

462
00:39:05,454 --> 00:39:12,103
So here's a simple example. We have this TRPC endpoint that was tRPC dot dollar sign tRPC dot tS.

463
00:39:12,103 --> 00:39:18,276
That's again how in TanStack we would get the data for that route, like the parameters, and pass things over.

464
00:39:18,606 --> 00:39:27,524
Now we are moving to the nitrous index because we're moving to nitro as the thing that resolves these routes, which means we need our own way to get the event that is

465
00:39:27,918 --> 00:39:30,820
the thing that happened on that route, so we can pass that to our handler.

466
00:39:31,278 --> 00:39:42,016
So we have this H3 event helper that we ripped out of TanStack and out of Nitro and re-exported. So we could use it for our mix of nitro routes and Tanstack

467
00:39:42,016 --> 00:39:52,176
routes. So you'll see here the route tree, which again, I actually like having it be code gened and committed because you can look at the output of this and know what changed.

468
00:39:52,256 --> 00:39:55,856
And here you see all of our API endpoints got removed.

469
00:39:56,206 --> 00:40:05,936
Routes, API uploading routes, API stripe, status, server version, checkout sync, chat, all of our API routes were removed from TanStack entirely.

470
00:40:06,158 --> 00:40:12,496
And then here in the VT config where we actually configure Nitro, we pass a server dir now.

471
00:40:12,736 --> 00:40:13,456
Very important.

472
00:40:13,710 --> 00:40:20,016
The server directory is us specifying where the server functions to find the way Nitro likes are.

473
00:40:20,366 --> 00:40:30,244
And by putting this here, we are able to define our own nitro functions outside of defining them in Tanstack, which is kind of funny because if we go back to what my previous plan was,

474
00:40:30,574 --> 00:40:36,260
we've almost recreated the V plus Hano setup that I was talking about wanting way, way back.

475
00:40:36,656 --> 00:40:39,856
But instead, it's all being done inside of Tanstack Start.

476
00:40:40,016 --> 00:40:45,892
Instead of doing this on the outside of the framework, we're doing it inside of the framework, which does admittedly require quite a few hacks.

477
00:40:46,254 --> 00:40:54,564
The fact that we had to patch package Tanstack Start in order to expose the event helper so we'd have access to that is a little annoying.

478
00:40:54,926 --> 00:40:57,412
Ideally, nobody would need this get H3 event thing,

479
00:40:58,158 --> 00:41:02,916
but we do, because we couldn't use the Tanstack routes at the scale we're shipping.

480
00:41:04,230 --> 00:41:12,736
And I didn't realize how many other random hellish things we had to do for handling our analytics proxy because it would use the wrong content encoding when we proxy.

481
00:41:12,816 --> 00:41:19,936
God, there's so many of these little things. Like anybody telling you that our next JS solution was a bunch of hacks and we could just move off it and get rid of the hacks.

482
00:41:20,622 --> 00:41:25,508
Now, Any large enough application will have hacks in every direction always.

483
00:41:25,838 --> 00:41:30,276
We traded one set that were scarier for a set that is overall less scary.

484
00:41:30,670 --> 00:41:38,564
But there are still things like this, where we have to serve this file from our public directory instead of serving it through this redirect because of the way the content encoding would break.

485
00:41:39,278 --> 00:41:42,596
And now we get to this max duration 799 thing.

486
00:41:43,022 --> 00:41:44,900
There's a good chance you're looking at that and thinking,

487
00:41:45,550 --> 00:41:46,436
what the fuck is that?

488
00:41:47,022 --> 00:41:48,016
That's a really good question.

489
00:41:48,366 --> 00:41:56,416
At the time we built most of T3 chat, the maximum duration for how long a request could be in flight for on a VerseL server before it is killed was 800 seconds.

490
00:41:56,750 --> 00:42:01,252
So if you have an AI generation that takes 900 seconds, it's going to get cut off before then.

491
00:42:01,582 --> 00:42:04,772
The default is 300, but the actual hard max is 800.

492
00:42:05,262 --> 00:42:06,724
So why do we pick 799?

493
00:42:07,296 --> 00:42:09,988
Not as simple to answer as you might hope.

494
00:42:10,416 --> 00:42:17,536
Going back to the next JS version, you will notice that I put this on the API chat route.

495
00:42:18,222 --> 00:42:19,684
The max duration 799.

496
00:42:20,462 --> 00:42:22,032
The reason I describe here.

497
00:42:22,286 --> 00:42:26,032
This breaks out the API chat route from being bundled in fluid compute.

498
00:42:26,286 --> 00:42:29,552
Other functions resolve faster and get in the way of paralyzing this particular route.

499
00:42:29,774 --> 00:42:31,632
This is a hack that was suggested by Malta.

500
00:42:31,952 --> 00:42:42,432
One of the important pieces of how fluid compute works is it is a strategy to allow for many requests to be resolved by one node or one instance of Lambda running node at the same time.

501
00:42:42,670 --> 00:42:50,820
Normally the way Lambda works is a request comes in, it gets resolved, and then that Lambda takes the next request once it's done with the previous one, but it only does one at a time.

502
00:42:51,278 --> 00:42:59,792
So if your request logic is spending a lot of time waiting, that node is just sitting there doing nothing until the next like

503
00:43:00,270 --> 00:43:02,112
piece of whatever it's supposed to do comes in.

504
00:43:02,350 --> 00:43:09,952
So if you're doing an AI generation and you're hitting the open AI API for Chat GPT, whenever you're not getting a new token, you're sitting there doing nothing.

505
00:43:10,318 --> 00:43:18,832
This is why fluid compute is awesome, because it lets you reuse that less used compute that would just be sitting there doing nothing, waiting for the next like chunk to come from the API.

506
00:43:19,054 --> 00:43:21,188
And you can use that compute to resolve other requests.

507
00:43:21,550 --> 00:43:27,712
So five people can be requesting at the same time, and all of that is going through at once.

508
00:43:28,078 --> 00:43:33,540
Super nice. Let's you pay way less money and take much more advantage of your servers.

509
00:43:33,902 --> 00:43:36,672
When your request only took like 100 milliseconds, this didn't matter.

510
00:43:36,942 --> 00:43:40,036
Now that the request can take 800 seconds, it matters a lot more.

511
00:43:40,430 --> 00:43:42,532
So why did I pick 799 here?

512
00:43:42,912 --> 00:43:45,572
Because every other route was set to 800.

513
00:43:45,902 --> 00:43:53,632
If you have two different routes that have different max durations, the way that Fluid Compute is provisioned and deployed would

514
00:43:53,632 --> 00:44:02,756
allow for all of the ones that share a max duration to be served together and the ones that don't have a shared max duration to be broken off on their own.

515
00:44:03,118 --> 00:44:13,188
So effectively, this guarantees all API slash chat requests go to the same set of lambdas and all other requests go to a different set.

516
00:44:13,518 --> 00:44:21,220
The setting of this one second difference breaks out the bundles so that they are separate and different routes are hitting different boxes.

517
00:44:21,454 --> 00:44:28,900
This was great because it meant that the chat deduping and handling all of the chat requests on one lambda could scale way better.

518
00:44:29,166 --> 00:44:33,508
Because now, like let's say hypothetically, one of these boxes can handle a thousand requests.

519
00:44:34,112 --> 00:44:44,484
If a third of them are chat requests that take 800 seconds up to, and the rest are all TRPC calls that take 50 milliseconds, that is not an optimal use of fluid compute.

520
00:44:44,752 --> 00:44:54,352
Ideally, the box that can handle long requests is handling a lot of them, and its slots for how many requests it can handle aren't being wasted by something that does more I.O.

521
00:44:54,512 --> 00:44:57,188
per second and is going for way fewer seconds.

522
00:44:57,550 --> 00:45:04,452
It's way easier for the fluid compute like routing layer to know how to route things with this little hint.

523
00:45:05,358 --> 00:45:13,712
And we have basically lost that here because as far as I understand, putting this max duration here is making all of our APIs bundled under that.

524
00:45:13,838 --> 00:45:22,912
So we no longer have the chat endpoint split off from the other API endpoints, but we do have the API endpoints split off separately from the TanStack start ones,

525
00:45:22,992 --> 00:45:30,948
which is a good enough solution for now because we've really reduced the number of those API calls that happened. Most of everything now goes through convex and the chat endpoint.

526
00:45:31,152 --> 00:45:38,912
That said, it would be nice if we could still change the duration on just chat so it would be bundled separately and maximize our fluid compute usage.

527
00:45:39,150 --> 00:45:41,924
Let me actually go check how our fluid compute use is right now.

528
00:45:42,414 --> 00:45:50,660
I wanted to see how this change affected our fluid compute utilization, but uh they got rid of the dashboard that I would normally check that in in Verse L, so I can't show you.

529
00:45:51,374 --> 00:45:51,972
Annoying.

530
00:45:52,430 --> 00:45:54,020
So now for the last piece

531
00:45:54,766 --> 00:45:55,524
SSR.

532
00:45:56,366 --> 00:46:02,564
The easiest way to know that we have SSR now is when you go to an INCOG tab and load, you'll see the skeleton

533
00:46:03,662 --> 00:46:04,832
that I don't like very much.

534
00:46:06,542 --> 00:46:11,172
See the bottom there where it shows the login state and then it vanishes and becomes the login button.

535
00:46:11,790 --> 00:46:14,372
See the sidebar here where it has the fake empty chats.

536
00:46:15,374 --> 00:46:22,788
That's all because we are now rendering a template and then filling in the pieces once the server finishes them or the client does for different routes.

537
00:46:23,118 --> 00:46:31,812
And for certain things, like when you load a specific chat for the first time, we render it on the server to get as much of the data there as possible before you start loading the page on your side.

538
00:46:32,142 --> 00:46:34,352
Hypothetically, this should be really good.

539
00:46:34,670 --> 00:46:35,684
And in a lot of ways, it is.

540
00:46:36,078 --> 00:46:44,964
Like when I'm navigating around here, it feels good. And when I refresh, it loads pretty much instantly, and you get a better state for your chat history when it happens.

541
00:46:45,550 --> 00:46:52,068
It is in a lot of ways good, but I am still skeptical of how much we benefit from server rendering at all.

542
00:46:52,494 --> 00:46:54,592
I am the most skeptical one on the team.

543
00:46:54,752 --> 00:46:56,644
I don't think this is necessary.

544
00:46:57,232 --> 00:47:04,004
I am highly considering pulling us off of it. But it is cool that the first load has like a better state overall.

545
00:47:04,558 --> 00:47:09,952
It has its catches. Like the way that the names load in was not right originally. We've fixed it for the most part.

546
00:47:10,158 --> 00:47:14,820
It is really cool that we're sending down a better payload from the server than we previously were.

547
00:47:15,118 --> 00:47:23,108
But at the same time, I think I would prefer if we had one HTML file serving the majority of our site and just did everything on client side where it made sense to.

548
00:47:23,312 --> 00:47:26,532
But again, I'm not the one who made these decisions.

549
00:47:26,894 --> 00:47:29,924
I'm not really the tech lead for T3 chat anymore.

550
00:47:30,112 --> 00:47:35,872
I have to run the company and do this channel and do everything else that I have to do in a given day.

551
00:47:36,110 --> 00:47:39,392
My team made these decisions because I hired my team to make good decisions.

552
00:47:39,632 --> 00:47:43,012
This is the decision that they wanted, and I have argued it plenty.

553
00:47:43,342 --> 00:47:48,740
They are still confident it was the right one. And I will continue to back them until we as a team decide otherwise.

554
00:47:49,102 --> 00:47:58,084
And at least we're more stable than Twitch chat. It's funny because I've previously bragged that Twitch chat is proof that we know how to do quickly updating, complex chat UIs.

555
00:47:58,414 --> 00:48:00,612
And now Twitch chat is down literally as I am filming.

556
00:48:02,030 --> 00:48:05,472
Our move off of Next.js isn't because Next.js is bad.

557
00:48:05,552 --> 00:48:08,676
It's not because we are super excited about Tanstack start.

558
00:48:08,992 --> 00:48:17,232
It is for the same reasons that it was when we were first planning it back in February, which is that the way I'm hacking Next.js to behave how we want isn't ideal.

559
00:48:17,486 --> 00:48:20,512
We don't want to wait for the next team to make the single page app experience better.

560
00:48:20,750 --> 00:48:23,748
So we want to move to something that has a better single page app experience.

561
00:48:24,032 --> 00:48:32,452
We still want to maintain the cohesion of the front end back end deploying together. So a framework that allows us to have one package JSON that pulls the two together is very enticing to us.

562
00:48:32,974 --> 00:48:35,364
And we really like Tanner in the way that he builds.

563
00:48:35,726 --> 00:48:39,748
We have found TanSec Dart to be, for the most part, flexible enough for our use case.

564
00:48:40,110 --> 00:48:42,912
It is still very early, which scares the shit out of me.

565
00:48:43,054 --> 00:48:49,572
And a lot of the problems that we've had are us going headfirst into the fire and bringing out the solutions for everybody else.

566
00:48:49,872 --> 00:48:56,672
Like having to move all of our routes off of TanStack to Nitro is the type of thing that would have kept me from making the move in the first place.

567
00:48:56,752 --> 00:49:00,612
If I was told that that's something we'd have to do, I would have blocked the Tanstack start move.

568
00:49:01,102 --> 00:49:02,032
But now that it's

569
00:49:02,032 --> 00:49:04,836
done, we have a code base that we understand much better.

570
00:49:05,262 --> 00:49:10,432
A lot of the bugs we were dealing with before around off-state stuff are much clearer and easier for us to find.

571
00:49:10,670 --> 00:49:19,908
We get to leave behind all the backwards compat and monolithic chaos that was Next.js with something that's quite a bit more minimal and newer and a little bit more flexible for the things we need.

572
00:49:20,112 --> 00:49:22,352
We love the new routing stuff in Tanstack Router.

573
00:49:22,574 --> 00:49:23,684
It's significantly better.

574
00:49:23,918 --> 00:49:29,860
And now my team, the people who actually run this every day, are the ones who own the implementation details, not me.

575
00:49:30,286 --> 00:49:35,952
That's arguably the biggest win here is that the code is no longer Theo's code being fixed by the team.

576
00:49:36,174 --> 00:49:37,952
The code is now the team's code.

577
00:49:38,254 --> 00:49:43,552
Mark and Julius worked really hard on these changes. Julius in particular went crazy pulling this all off.

578
00:49:43,758 --> 00:49:45,444
I get to wipe my hands clean now.

579
00:49:45,646 --> 00:49:46,352
I'm out.

580
00:49:46,670 --> 00:49:50,272
This isn't moving next to Tanstack, as many of you guys are going to brand it.

581
00:49:50,446 --> 00:49:54,628
This isn't Theo getting excited about the shiny new thing. In fact, I probably wouldn't have even chosen Tanstack.

582
00:49:55,072 --> 00:50:03,952
This is the move away from Theo's hacky pile of solutions, forcing Next.js to behave how I wanted towards something that the team better understands

583
00:50:04,032 --> 00:50:08,228
and controls that gets us closer to our long-term vision of how we want T3 chat.

584
00:50:08,512 --> 00:50:15,012
We traded one set of hacks for another, we traded one framework for another, but in the end, the majority of the code in the repo didn't change.

585
00:50:15,342 --> 00:50:20,004
We are still deep in it, trying to make the best possible chat experience.

586
00:50:20,590 --> 00:50:22,192
My team can now debug more easily.

587
00:50:22,414 --> 00:50:25,188
The tech we're building on feels a little better overall.

588
00:50:25,486 --> 00:50:32,612
But in the end, this honestly kind of deepened my appreciation for Next and the fact that I could abuse it the ways I did, and it worked for the most part.

589
00:50:32,942 --> 00:50:34,592
It also, like, man.

590
00:50:35,552 --> 00:50:44,516
The scaling and large code base size issues we've been hitting with Tan Stack, like the Nitro stuff, just it shows how early these things are.

591
00:50:45,070 --> 00:50:46,468
And they really are.

592
00:50:46,894 --> 00:50:52,452
If you're here looking for advice on what stack to build your next project with, I got nothing for you.

593
00:50:52,974 --> 00:50:57,700
I've had enough problems with all of these stacks that there's no easy, obvious recommendation.

594
00:50:58,158 --> 00:50:58,628
Yeah.

595
00:50:59,054 --> 00:51:03,632
There's no real lesson to take from this other than the inner workings are interesting.

596
00:51:03,950 --> 00:51:13,152
And once you ship at a certain size and scale, your team and their needs are way more important in the real world than any particular framework will tell you.

597
00:51:13,454 --> 00:51:20,164
If anybody thinks our problems can be solved with a simple move to Laravel or a simple move to TanStack Start or some other thing,

598
00:51:20,686 --> 00:51:24,388
they're either lying to you because they don't know what they're talking about or they have something to sell you.

599
00:51:24,686 --> 00:51:25,540
Don't trust them.

600
00:51:25,838 --> 00:51:29,764
I'm not here to sell you anything other than a really good AI chat app that you should definitely check out.

601
00:51:30,254 --> 00:51:38,352
And if you're thinking that this is something to do with like the Vercel exploit or any drama on that side, and you ignored the fact that we have been building all of this on Vercel

602
00:51:38,512 --> 00:51:46,724
the whole time, I don't know what to tell you. I hope you start listening a little more closely because obviously this is not us disliking Vercel.

603
00:51:47,356 --> 00:51:53,152
This is us having very specific technical requirements that no framework serves properly.

604
00:51:53,358 --> 00:51:58,180
So we're trying to find the one that we can bastardize the best to give us the experience we want to have.

605
00:51:58,830 --> 00:52:07,472
Right now, it seems like Tanztech Start probably will get there, both because it has a lot of really good pieces, and I feel like we can influence the developers building it most

606
00:52:07,662 --> 00:52:09,284
to get it where we need it to be.

607
00:52:09,774 --> 00:52:14,788
But man, nothing works how we want it to, and this has been a hell of a move.

608
00:52:15,152 --> 00:52:21,220
Hopefully, the next video will be about how exciting our new features are that we get to ship because we're now unblocked and all the tech stuff.

609
00:52:21,422 --> 00:52:23,780
But I have a bad feeling we're going to hit more edges on the way there.

610
00:52:24,142 --> 00:52:26,112
Wish us luck. It's gonna be hellish.

611
00:52:26,414 --> 00:52:28,580
And until next time, peace nerds.

